            <ArticleTitle>Bochdalek-type Diaphragmatic Hernia Leading to High-grade Kidney Obstruction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e17-e18</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0090-4295(16)30538-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.urology.2016.08.025</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 94-year-old female presented with sharp right flank pain. Imaging demonstrated herniation of the right renal pelvis and proximal ureter into a large diaphragmatic hernia. She underwent ureteral stent placement with resolution of her symptoms. Congenital diaphragmatic hernias can cause a variety of pulmonary, cardiac, and gastrointestinal symptoms. This is 1 of only 3 cases in the literature of unilateral kidney obstruction due to herniation of the renal pelvis and proximal ureter into a Bochdalek-type diaphragmatic hernia. Ureteral stenting is a good option to decompress the kidney. Hernia reduction and primary diaphragm repair remain the definitive treatment.</AbstractText>
